---
import ShareIcon from "@/assets/icons/share.svg"
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import MetaPage from "@/components/MetaPage.astro"
import { campaignStatus } from "@/data/campaign"
import { sourceGroups } from "@/data/sources"
const researchDate = "20 September 2026"
const sources = sourceGroups.map((group) => ({
  title: group.titleEn,
  sources: group.sources.map((source) => ({
    href: source.href,
    label: source.labelEn,
  })),
}))
const concerns = [
  {
    title: "Everyone, not just suspects.",
    text: "Providers must prepare reporting data for all customers before it is even clear who will be reported. No specific reason or suspicion is required. We reject this precautionary collection.",
  },
  {
    title: "A profile over the years.",
    text: "Unlike bank-account reporting, crypto-asset reports include units and transaction counts. Year after year, this reveals activity even though current balances are not reported.",
  },
  {
    title: "A data breach cannot be undone.",
    text: "A provider breach can expose the link between your name and blockchain transactions. Once that link spreads, it is hard to take back. It can reveal further payments and make people targets.",
  },
  {
    title: "The purpose keeps expanding.",
    text: "Under the directive, exchanged data may be used for customs, anti-money-laundering purposes and, under certain conditions, sanctions as well as taxation. Each new purpose needs its own clear limits.",
  },
  {
    title: "Errors travel too.",
    text: "An incorrect tax residence, valuation or interpretation of a transfer can be passed to other countries. The more bodies involved, the harder it becomes to correct the record.",
  },
  {
    title: "Ten years without an explanation.",
    text: "Providers delete reported crypto-asset data ten years after the reporting period. For corresponding bank-account data, it is five years. The legislative materials do not explain the difference.",
  },
]
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Am I affected just because I own bitcoin?",
    answer:
      "Only if a crypto-asset provider subject to Austrian reporting obligations is involved, such as an exchange or broker based in Austria. DAC8 does not report people who exclusively hold bitcoin in self-custody and do not use such a provider.",
  },
  {
    question: "Will my own wallet address be reported?",
    answer:
      "No. Wallet addresses are not part of the annual DAC8 report. But the value and units transferred to addresses not known to belong to another provider are reported. This generally includes your own wallet. The address and its link to you may still remain in the provider’s records.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are exchanges of bitcoin for another crypto-asset reported?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Value, units and transaction counts are reported on both sides, even though in Austria such an exchange usually does not constitute a taxable disposal for individuals.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is DAC8 an asset registry?",
    answer:
      "No. It reports annual activity aggregates, not current balances or total wealth. Combined with account registries, provider records and blockchain analysis, they could nevertheless reveal a detailed picture of a person’s assets.",
  },
  {
    question: "Will transactions before 2026 be reported retroactively?",
    answer:
      "No. The first reporting period starts on 1 January 2026. A retroactive obligation would require a new law. Neither DAC9 nor the current proposal to recast the directive provides for this.",
  },
  {
    question: "Will my data be deleted when I close my account?",
    answer:
      "Not immediately. Under Austria’s Financial Markets Anti-Money Laundering Act, providers must retain necessary identification and transaction records for ten years after the business relationship ends.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can every cryptocurrency payment be traced?",
    answer:
      "No. A known Bitcoin address can reveal further payments on the blockchain. It is not always possible to reliably identify who made them or why. Individual Lightning payments are not recorded on the public blockchain.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do DAC9 and DAC10 mean more crypto surveillance?",
    answer:
      "Not automatically. DAC9 concerns minimum taxation of large corporate groups, not individual crypto users. The recast proposed in June 2026, often called DAC10, is not yet law and retains the existing crypto reporting fields.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are you against taxation or anti-money-laundering measures?",
    answer:
      "No. Authorities should investigate tax evasion and money laundering in targeted cases and obtain the data they need. We oppose collecting data on all customers as a precaution, without specific suspicion.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is DAC8 unlawful?",
    answer:
      "We believe Austria’s collection covering all customers violates fundamental rights and seek judicial review. Whether these specific obligations are proportionate is for the courts to decide.",
  },
  {
    question: "What happens to my data on this website?",
    answer:
      "Reading the site and subscribing to RSS require no registration. Our privacy notice explains the technical data involved in hosting and what is processed when you contact us or donate.",
  },
]
---

<Layout
  sections={[
  { id: "was-ist-dac8", label: "What is DAC8?" },
  { id: "gemeldet", label: "What gets reported" },
  { id: "problem", label: "Why it is a problem" },
  { id: "ausblick", label: "What might come next" },
  { id: "forderungen", label: "Our demands" },
  { id: "rechtsweg", label: "Our legal strategy" },
  { id: "faq", label: "Questions and answers" },
  { id: "quellen", label: "Sources" },
]}
>
  <MetaPage
    slot="head"
    title="Understanding DAC8"
    description="What DAC8 and Austria’s Crypto-Asset Reporting Obligations Act require, what gets reported and how we are challenging it. With primary sources."
  />
  <article class="campaign dossier">
    <header class="hero">
      <h1>What DAC8 requires. <span>And what it does not.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        What data about you gets reported, what your provider keeps and why we
        are challenging it.
      </p>
      <nav class="hero-actions" aria-label="Get started">
        <a class="button primary" href="#gemeldet">What gets reported</a
        ><a class="button" href="#rechtsweg">Our legal strategy</a>
      </nav>
      <dl class="glance" aria-label="Current position">
        <dt>Law in force</dt>
        <dd>since 1 January 2026</dd>
        <dt>First report</dt>
        <dd>for 2026, due by 31 July 2027</dd>
      </dl>
    </header>
    <section id="was-ist-dac8" aria-labelledby="was-heading">
      <h2 id="was-heading">What is DAC8?</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        DAC8 is an EU directive extending automatic exchange of tax information
        between EU countries to crypto-assets. Austria implemented it through
        the Crypto-Asset Reporting Obligations Act (Krypto-MPfG), which has
        applied since 1 January 2026.
      </p>
      <ol class="flow" aria-label="Where the data goes">
        <li>
          <strong>You</strong
          ><span
            >use an exchange or broker with reporting obligations in Austria</span
          >
        </li>
        <li class="accent">
          <strong>The provider</strong
          ><span
            >prepares identities and annual aggregates for all customers and
            reports once a year</span
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The tax authority</strong
          ><span>receives, analyses and stores the report</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Other EU countries</strong
          ><span>receive the data if you are tax-resident there</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="callout">
        <strong>Austria’s additional requirement:</strong>
        Before it is established who will be reported, the provider must prepare
        reporting data for all customers, all transactions and all
        crypto-assets. The directive does not require this.<small
          >Section 20(1) Krypto-MPfG.</small
        >
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="gemeldet" aria-labelledby="gemeldet-heading">
      <h2 id="gemeldet-heading">What gets reported, and what does not</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        The report contains identities and annual aggregates, not individual
        transactions.
      </p>
      <div class="compare">
        <div>
          <h3>Included in the report</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Name, address, tax residence, tax identification number and date
              of birth
            </li>
            <li>
              For each crypto-asset and activity category: euro value, units and
              transaction count, separated into purchases, sales, exchanges and
              transfers
            </li>
            <li>
              Value and units transferred to addresses not associated with a
              known provider, generally including your own wallet
            </li>
            <li>
              Activities that often have no tax consequences, such as
              crypto-to-crypto exchanges
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="absent">
          <h3>Not included in the report</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Wallet addresses and transaction identifiers</li>
            <li>Individual transactions, timestamps or counterparties</li>
            <li>Current balances or total wealth</li>
            <li>Activities before 1 January 2026</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>
          <strong>The timetable</strong>
          <p>
            The first reporting period is 2026. Providers report to the tax
            authority by 31 July 2027. Austria exchanges the information with
            other EU countries by 30 September 2027.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What the provider retains</strong>
          <p>
            Your provider can link your name to wallet addresses and individual
            transactions. These details are outside the annual DAC8 report but
            may remain in its records, depending on the service.
            <a
              href="https://www.ris.bka.gv.at/GeltendeFassung.wxe?Abfrage=Bundesnormen&amp;Gesetzesnummer=20009769"
              >Section 21 FM-GwG</a
            >
            requires, independently of DAC8, necessary identification and
            transaction records to be kept for ten years after the business
            relationship ends or an occasional transaction.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading">Why it is a problem</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Your data is prepared and stored for years even without suspicion. This
        intrudes on your private life and exposes you to errors, breaches and
        misuse.
      </p>
      <ul class="facts">
        {concerns.map(concern => <li><strong>{concern.title}</strong><p>{concern.text}</p></li>)}
      </ul>
      <h3 id="zahlungsprofile">When payments reveal everyday life</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        Paying with cryptocurrencies can leave traces of everyday life.
        Bitcoin’s on-chain transactions remain public. When a provider links
        your name to an address, further payments may become attributable to
        you. With information about recipients, purchases, donations and
        recurring payments can
        <a href="https://petsymposium.org/popets/2018/popets-2018-0038.php"
          >reveal habits and relationships</a
        >.
      </p>
      <h3>Data breaches put people at risk</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        Stored links between identities and financial data are a valuable target
        for attackers. Long retention obligations keep these records available
        for years. In the wrong hands, they can be used for fraud, extortion or
        selecting victims.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The
        <a
          href="https://www.gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr/gendinfo/terrain/immersion/2026/la-strategie-de-la-gendarmerie-face-aux-cryptorapts-partie-2-2"
          >French Gendarmerie</a
        >
        reports that criminals use stolen customer data from crypto tax service
        Waltio to select targets. It describes kidnappings and physical
        violence. Even a mistaken assumption that someone owns substantial
        crypto assets can put them and their family at risk. Data minimisation
        and shorter retention periods also protect personal safety.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="ausblick" aria-labelledby="ausblick-heading">
      <h2 id="ausblick-heading">What might come next</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Reporting infrastructure can be expanded later. That is why we want
        effective limits on data collection now.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Requiring reports of wallet addresses, individual transactions or
        activities before 2026 would need a change in the law. None of this has
        been enacted, and the current proposal to recast the directive does not
        provide for it either.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="forderungen" aria-labelledby="forderungen-heading">
      <h2 id="forderungen-heading">Our demands</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        In a liberal democracy, authorities should be able to investigate
        specific suspicions, with a legal basis and independent oversight.
        Uninvolved people should not have their lives scrutinised as a
        precaution.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>Determine whose data must be reported first.</strong>
          No precautionary preparation of data for all customers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Collect only necessary data.</strong>
          The additional value of units and transaction counts must be
          demonstrated.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your own wallet is not a payment to someone else.</strong>
          Transfers to self-custodied addresses should be treated distinctly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep data no longer than necessary.</strong>
          Ten years instead of five needs a clear justification.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Strictly limit purpose, access and onward disclosure.</strong>
          Access must be traceable and subject to independent oversight.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your rights must work in practice.</strong>
          You must be able to access your data, correct errors and have
          unlawfully stored data deleted by every recipient.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Independently review benefits and risks.</strong>
          Regularly and publicly, so unnecessary intrusions can be ended.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="prose">
        <strong>Resistance can work.</strong>
        The challenge to data retention organised by AKVorrat, now
        epicenter.works, reached the Court of Justice through Austria’s
        Constitutional Court. In 2014, both the EU directive and Austrian rules
        fell. That success shows that even sweeping surveillance laws can be
        changed.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="rechtsweg" aria-labelledby="rechtsweg-heading">
      <h2 id="rechtsweg-heading">Our legal strategy</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        {campaignStatus.en.text}
      </p>
      <ol class="flow" aria-label="The legal route">
        <li>
          <strong>Provider</strong
          ><span>we document how a provider processes data for DAC8</span>
        </li>
        <li class="accent">
          <strong>Data Protection Authority</strong
          ><span>examines the complaint against the provider</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Federal Administrative Court</strong
          ><span>can review the Data Protection Authority’s decision</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Constitutional Court / CJEU</strong
          ><span
            >can review national law or the EU directive, respectively</span
          >
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="prose">
        The court decides whether to refer questions to the CJEU. The complaint
        does not automatically stop data collection; the duration of the
        proceedings is not yet known.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Whether and how individuals can take part will be clarified as the case
        develops. In the meantime, you can
        <a href="/en/donate/">help fund the case</a>
        and
        <a href="/en/updates/">follow its progress</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
      <h2 id="faq-heading">Questions and answers</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map(faq => <details><summary>{faq.question}</summary><p>{faq.answer}</p></details>)}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="quellen" class="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
      <h2 id="sources-heading">Sources</h2>
      <div class="source-groups">
        {sources.map(group => <div><h3>{group.title}</h3><ul>{group.sources.map(source => <li><a href={source.href}>{source.label}</a></li>)}</ul></div>)}
      </div>
      <p class="research-note">
        Research last updated: {researchDate}. Not individual legal advice.
      </p>
    </section>
    <aside class="closing" aria-labelledby="support-heading">
      <h2 id="support-heading">Support the case</h2>
      <div class="closing-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/en/donate/">Donate</a>
        <a class="button" href="/en/updates/">Updates</a>
        <button class="text-link" type="button" data-share>
          <ShareIcon />Share
        </button>
      </div>
      <p class="share-status" data-share-status aria-live="polite"></p>
    </aside>
  </article>
  <script>
    import { initializeShareButtons } from "@/lib/share"
    initializeShareButtons()
  </script>
</Layout>
